<|channel>thought
<channel|>We’ve reached a crossroads in personal computing: the tradeoff between seamless convenience and true technical control. Modern, ultra-thin laptops offer incredible portability and polished integration, but they often arrive as sealed black boxes. Once a single component fails, the entire machine is frequently destined for the landfill.

Choosing a repairable laptop means embracing a bit more bulk and complexity in exchange for longevity. It’s a shift from being a mere consumer of hardware to being an owner of it. [end of text]


